-- =============================================================================
-- BizApps Sales — Baseline Schema (v0.1.0)
-- =============================================================================
-- Creates the whole __mj_BizAppsSales table set per plans/bizapps-sales-master.md
-- §3 (type tables), §4 (data model), §5 (deal team), §9.5 (forecast snapshots).
--
-- BASELINE-IN-PLACE. Pre-production practice for this family: schema changes EDIT THIS FILE
-- rather than stacking fix-up migrations, then `scripts/rebuild-db.sh` rebuilds from zero and
-- CodeGen regenerates the half below the banner. That is only safe because rebuilding is routine.
-- Switch to additive-only at first publish.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN
--   * `__mj_CreatedAt` / `__mj_UpdatedAt` columns, and indexes on foreign key columns.
--     CodeGen creates both. Hand-adding them makes the generated half fight the hand-authored half.
--   * `INSERT`s for type-table vocabulary. Every seeded row ships as metadata under `metadata/<type>/`
--     with a hardcoded UUID, following bizapps-common's address-types pattern, so the rows are
--     diffable, re-pushable and identical across environments. A seed INSERT here would be none
--     of those things.
--   * CHECK constraints on domain vocabulary. See the note below — this is the app's second rule
--     and the constraint list in this file is the proof of it.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE TWO RULES THIS SCHEMA EXISTS TO MAKE STRUCTURAL (master plan §1)
--
-- 1. SALES NEVER COMPUTES MONEY. Every number comes back from `Orders.PreviewOrder`. That is why
--    DealLine carries `ResolvedUnitPrice` / `ResolvedExtendedAmount` / `PriceComponentsJSON` /
--    `PricedAt` as a WRITE-ONLY block populated from a PreviewOrder response, and why `Deal.Amount`
--    is a CACHED answer carrying its own provenance (`AmountIsComputed`, `AmountComputedAt`,
--    `AmountSourceHash`). The hash fingerprints the DealLine set the amount was computed from, so
--    the UI can say "this figure is stale, reprice" instead of showing a number nobody can trace.
--    Strip those three columns and `Amount` becomes a hand-edited field inside a month.
--
-- 2. DOMAIN VOCABULARY IS DATA, NEVER A CHECK CONSTRAINT. "Closed Won" is one organization's term
--    of art. Nine type tables carry the BEHAVIOUR FLAGS the engine branches on — `DealStatusType`
--    has `IsWon` / `IsLost` / `LocksDeal`, `ForecastCategoryType` has `IncludeInCommit`, and so on.
--    No server file may compare a status or stage NAME; a CI grep will prove it.
--
--    CHECK constraints therefore survive in this file ONLY for structural invariants:
--      * exactly-one-of foreign keys      (CK_DealTeamMember_EmployeeXorPerson)
--      * date ordering                    (CK_DealLine_ServicePeriodOrder, CK_ForecastSnapshot_PeriodOrder)
--      * non-negative / bounded numerics  (quantities, percentages, probabilities, scores)
--    Every one of those stays true no matter what an organization calls its stages.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- CROSS-APP REFERENCES — WHICH ARE REAL FKs AND WHY
--
-- HARD (the database enforces them):
--   → __mj.Company    Pipeline.CompanyID (NOT NULL, L-14), Deal.CompanyID, ForecastSnapshot.CompanyID
--   → __mj.Employee   SalesAccount.OwnerEmployeeID, SalesContact.OwnerEmployeeID,
--                     Deal.OwnerEmployeeID, DealTeamMember.EmployeeID, ForecastSnapshot.OwnerEmployeeID
--   → __mj.User       Deal.ClosedByUserID, DealStageEvent.ChangedByUserID
--   → __mj_BizAppsCommon.Organization   SalesAccount.ID   (the IsA link, §4.1)
--   → __mj_BizAppsCommon.Person         SalesContact.ID   (the IsA link, §4.1),
--                                       DealTeamMember.PersonID (D-6)
--
-- SOFT (plain UNIQUEIDENTIFIER, no FK) — because the target app's migrations may not have run.
-- This is DG-6, and it is the reason this baseline stands up with only bizapps-common present:
--   * DealLine.ProductID                → bizapps-orders catalog
--   * Deal.ContractID / RenewsContractID → bizapps-contracts (§4.4, L-15 — the reference points
--                                          DOWN the graph; there is deliberately no Contract.DealID,
--                                          because it is ONE contract to MANY deals)
--   * Deal.CurrencyID                   → no FK. Orders carries no currency columns at all (its D24
--                                          defers FX), so there is no settled target to point at
--                                          yet. Kept as a column because the master plan names it
--                                          and dropping it would silently decide a question that is
--                                          still open.
--   * Deal.CampaignID                   → no FK. Not present in MJ core at the version this
--                                          baseline was authored against; verify before promoting.
--
-- INSTALL-ORDER DEPENDENCY: bizapps-common MUST be installed before this migration. §4.1's two
-- IsA tables fail without it — deliberately, as the dependency check.
--
-- SQL Server is the source of truth; the PostgreSQL counterpart is produced via
-- @memberjunction/sql-converter. Production is PostgreSQL, so this file stays converter-friendly:
-- no reserved-word column names (see the ForecastSnapshot note), no T-SQL-only constructs.
-- =============================================================================


-- =============================================================================
-- 3. TYPE TABLES — the configuration layer (master plan §3, L-13)
-- =============================================================================
-- TYPE TABLES COME FIRST, and not only because the deal tables have foreign keys into them. They
-- are the app's extension mechanism: an organization renames, adds or reorders its vocabulary by
-- editing DATA, with no migration and no code change. Every one of them follows the same shape —
-- a stable `Code` the engine and metadata seeds key on, a `Name` the humans see, the behaviour
-- flags, then `DisplayRank` / `IsActive`.
--
-- `Code` is what makes this safe to reorganize: metadata seeds and any future policy JSON reference
-- the code, so renaming the display Name is a cosmetic act. Rename the Code and you have changed an
-- identifier — hence UNIQUE on it, so the mistake is loud.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.1 DealStatusType — the OUTCOME vocabulary, and the most load-bearing
--     type table in the app. `Sales.CloseDeal` is named for the outcome type
--     rather than for a hardcoded "won" precisely so that it can resolve its
--     entire behaviour from these flags (§7.2).
--
--     LocksDeal is the one to understand: when a deal enters a status carrying
--     it, the deal and its lines become IMMUTABLE at the entity-server level
--     (L-17, §7.3). That mirrors orders' journal-entry immutability and exists
--     for the same reason — the deal is now the provenance of a contract and an
--     order, so editing it retroactively falsifies both.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStatusType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    -- The behaviour flags. IsOpen/IsClosed are not redundant with each other in
    -- practice: "On Hold" is neither progressing nor concluded, and a rollup that
    -- wants "still live" differs from one that wants "not yet concluded".
    IsOpen BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsClosed BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsWon BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsLost BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    -- Freezes the deal, its lines and its team members. Enforced in
    -- DealEntityServer.Save(), never in the UI.
    LocksDeal BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealStatusType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealStatusType_Code UNIQUE (Code),
    -- STRUCTURAL, not vocabulary: a status cannot be simultaneously won and lost
    -- whatever an organization chooses to call it.
    --
    -- ⚠️ THE ONE STATEMENT IN THIS BASELINE THAT DOES NOT SURVIVE THE POSTGRESQL CONVERSION.
    -- Correct T-SQL, and correct on the source-of-truth platform — but see docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md KI-6.
    -- Verified by converting this file and applying the result to a real PostgreSQL 16 instance:
    --
    --   * `@memberjunction/sql-converter` maps the BIT column to BOOLEAN but leaves `= 1` alone, so it
    --     emits `"IsWon" = 1`. PostgreSQL has no implicit integer-to-boolean cast and refuses it:
    --     `operator does not exist: boolean = integer`.
    --   * The obvious portable rewrite does not work either. `CAST(IsWon AS int) + CAST(IsLost AS int)`
    --     is valid in BOTH dialects, but the converter's identifier-quoting pass mangles it into
    --     `"CAST"("IsWon" "AS" INTEGER)` — quoting the keywords as identifiers — which is a syntax error.
    --   * `IsWon + IsLost <= 1` is not an option: T-SQL refuses arithmetic on BIT outright.
    --
    -- AND THE CONVERTER REPORTS "0 errors" THROUGHOUT, because that count means "statements I could
    -- transform", not "output that applies". Nothing warns you; only applying the result does.
    --
    -- Every other statement in this file converts AND applies cleanly. Rather than contort the schema
    -- around a converter defect, this stays idiomatic T-SQL and the PG story is a documented one-line
    -- post-edit (`"IsWon" AND "IsLost"`). Revisit when the converter handles BIT comparisons.
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealStatusType_NotWonAndLost CHECK (NOT (IsWon = 1 AND IsLost = 1))
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.2 DealType — what KIND of motion this deal is, and what closing it owes.
--     RequiresRenewalSource is what makes the renewal chain enforceable: a
--     Renewal deal must name the contract it renews (Deal.RenewsContractID),
--     and §7.2 step 4 routes it to Contracts.RenewTerm instead of
--     Contracts.CreateFromDeal on that basis.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    RequiresContract BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    RequiresRenewalSource BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    -- FK added in §5 below: DealType and Pipeline reference each other (a pipeline
    -- has a default deal type; a deal type has a default pipeline), so one of the two
    -- constraints has to be added after both tables exist.
    DefaultPipelineID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealType_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.3 DealRole — the roles internal people hold on a deal (§5, L-16).
--
--     Two flags here do real work. IsOwnerRole identifies the single role that
--     defines ownership; exactly one member per deal may hold it, and
--     Deal.OwnerEmployeeID is the server-maintained stamp of whoever does.
--     AllowsMultiplePerDeal is enforced SERVER-SIDE FROM THE FLAG, not hardcoded:
--     two sales engineers on a deal is normal, two owners is not.
--
--     DefaultAttributionPct feeds the trap named in §5.2 — a deal with an AE, an
--     SE and an SDR has three rows, and summing Deal.Amount across them
--     TRIPLE-COUNTS the deal. Every by-rep rollup must either filter to the owner
--     role or weight by AttributionPct.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealRole (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsOwnerRole BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    AllowsMultiplePerDeal BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    DefaultAttributionPct DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,
    IsQuotaCarrying BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealRole PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealRole_Code UNIQUE (Code),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealRole_DefaultAttributionPct
        CHECK (DefaultAttributionPct IS NULL OR (DefaultAttributionPct >= 0 AND DefaultAttributionPct <= 100))
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.4 ForecastCategoryType — how a deal rolls into the forecast.
--     The three Include* flags are read directly by the §9.3 measures; a
--     forecast query that compared a category NAME would be the exact
--     violation the CI grep is there to catch.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.ForecastCategoryType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IncludeInCommit BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IncludeInBestCase BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IncludeInPipeline BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_ForecastCategoryType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_ForecastCategoryType_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.5 LossReason — why a deal was lost. Loss reason is the app's ONLY
--     mandatory field (§7.2) and the friction is deliberate: loss reasons are
--     the highest-value and most consistently-skipped data in any CRM.
--
--     `Category` groups reasons for reporting and is free text rather than a
--     further type table — it is a reporting rollup of a vocabulary that is
--     already data, and a type table for a type table's grouping earns nothing.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.LossReason (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    Category NVARCHAR(100) NULL,
    -- When 1, Sales.CloseDeal refuses the close without LossNotes.
    RequiresNotes BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsCompetitive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_LossReason PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_LossReason_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.6 LeadSourceType — where the deal, account or contact came from.
--     AttributionWindowDays is the per-source horizon over which a touch still
--     counts as the origin, so first-touch attribution is configuration rather
--     than a constant buried in a report.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.LeadSourceType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsInbound BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsPaid BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    AttributionWindowDays INT NULL,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_LeadSourceType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_LeadSourceType_Code UNIQUE (Code),
    CONSTRAINT CK_LeadSourceType_AttributionWindowDays
        CHECK (AttributionWindowDays IS NULL OR AttributionWindowDays >= 0)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.7 LifecycleStageType — where a PERSON or ORGANIZATION sits on the journey
--     from stranger to evangelist. This is the table that makes "Lead" a STAGE
--     rather than an entity (L-3): a lead is a common.Person carrying a
--     LifecycleStageTypeID, not a second identity table for humans. MJ already
--     ships duplicate detection and record merge for the messy inbound case.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.LifecycleStageType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsMarketingQualified BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsSalesQualified BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsCustomer BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_LifecycleStageType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_LifecycleStageType_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.8 BuyingRoleType — the role a CUSTOMER-side contact plays in the buying
--     committee. InfluenceWeight lets a deal-health model weigh a champion
--     against an end user without hardcoding either name.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.BuyingRoleType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsDecisionMaker BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsBlocker BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    InfluenceWeight DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_BuyingRoleType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_BuyingRoleType_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.9 AccountType — what an account IS to us commercially. The three flags are
--     not mutually exclusive on purpose: a Partner that also buys is both, and
--     forcing a single-valued "type" is what makes CRMs grow a second field
--     called "is also a partner".
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.AccountType (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsCustomer BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsProspect BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsPartner BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_AccountType PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_AccountType_Code UNIQUE (Code)
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 4. IDENTITY — IsA extensions, not new tables (master plan §4.1, L-3)
-- =============================================================================
-- Both tables below are IsA CHILDREN sharing the parent's UUID: the primary key IS the foreign key.
-- A SalesAccount and its Organization are ONE record with one identity, so a customer that is also
-- a vendor, a member and an event attendee stays one row in the Person/Organization graph while
-- each app keeps its own attributes. CodeGen is told about the relationship explicitly in
-- codegen-schema-info.json, which is what makes it set Entity.ParentID, mirror the parent's fields
-- as virtual fields on the child, and JOIN the parent in the child's base view.
--
-- ⚠️ THE LANDMINE, recorded here because the schema cannot express it. MJ's IsA defaults to
-- DISJOINT — one child type per parent record — governed by `__mj.Entity.AllowMultipleSubtypes`.
-- On common's Person and Organization that flag is currently 0. Sales is the FIRST app to extend
-- either (accounting extends MJ: Companies; orders extends its own Product and OrderLine), so the
-- disjoint default chains CORRECTLY today and these tables are safe to ship. It must be flipped to
-- 1 in bizapps-common BEFORE a second app extends Person or Organization — ATS and certification
-- both intend to, and the same human is routinely a sales contact, an applicant and a member. The
-- failure mode if it is not flipped is silent mis-chaining at runtime, not an error.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.1 SalesAccount IsA __mj_BizAppsCommon.Organization
--
--     AnnualRevenueBand and EmployeeCountBand are deliberately BANDS, NOT
--     NUMBERS. A rep's guess stored as $4,750,000 is false precision that gets
--     treated as fact three quarters later; "$1M–$5M" cannot be.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.SalesAccount (
    -- The IsA link: same ID as the Organization. Not a separate surrogate key.
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,

    OwnerEmployeeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    AccountTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    LifecycleStageTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    LeadSourceTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- A LABEL, not a routing engine (open question 2; recommendation: label).
    -- Territory assignment as a rules engine is a product in its own right and is
    -- on the cross-app plan's explicit not-doing list.
    Territory NVARCHAR(100) NULL,
    Tier NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    ICPFitScore INT NULL,
    IndustryCode NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    EmployeeCountBand NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    AnnualRevenueBand NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    HealthStatus NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    FirstClosedWonDate DATE NULL,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_SalesAccount PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesAccount_Organization FOREIGN KEY (ID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsCommon.Organization (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesAccount_OwnerEmployee FOREIGN KEY (OwnerEmployeeID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Employee (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesAccount_AccountType FOREIGN KEY (AccountTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.AccountType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesAccount_LifecycleStageType FOREIGN KEY (LifecycleStageTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LifecycleStageType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesAccount_LeadSourceType FOREIGN KEY (LeadSourceTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LeadSourceType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT CK_SalesAccount_ICPFitScore
        CHECK (ICPFitScore IS NULL OR (ICPFitScore >= 0 AND ICPFitScore <= 100))
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4.2 SalesContact IsA __mj_BizAppsCommon.Person
--
--     OptedOutOfOutreach / DoNotContactReason live HERE rather than on the
--     Person because consent is a sales-outreach fact, not an identity fact,
--     and the Person graph is shared with apps that have no business reading it.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.SalesContact (
    -- The IsA link: same ID as the Person.
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,

    OwnerEmployeeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    LifecycleStageTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- The contact's DEFAULT buying role. The per-deal role lives on DealContactRole
    -- (§4.5) — one contact holds different roles on different deals, which is why
    -- that is a junction rather than only a field here.
    BuyingRoleTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    LeadSourceTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    Seniority NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    OptedOutOfOutreach BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    DoNotContactReason NVARCHAR(500) NULL,
    LastEngagedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_SalesContact PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesContact_Person FOREIGN KEY (ID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsCommon.Person (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesContact_OwnerEmployee FOREIGN KEY (OwnerEmployeeID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Employee (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesContact_LifecycleStageType FOREIGN KEY (LifecycleStageTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LifecycleStageType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesContact_BuyingRoleType FOREIGN KEY (BuyingRoleTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.BuyingRoleType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_SalesContact_LeadSourceType FOREIGN KEY (LeadSourceTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LeadSourceType (ID)
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 5. PIPELINES — owned by a company (master plan §4.2, L-14)
-- =============================================================================

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5.1 Pipeline
--
--     CompanyID IS NOT NULL, and that single decision is what makes every
--     rollup in §9 sliceable by company for free. It also matches how BC
--     actually sells — several operating companies, each with its own motions.
--     `Code` is unique PER COMPANY, not globally, for the same reason: two
--     operating companies may each run a pipeline they both call "NEWBIZ".
--
--     CloseWonPolicy is JSON, and that is a considered choice rather than
--     laziness. It declares the DEFAULT OUTCOME of winning — create a contract
--     or not, which contract type, where subscription lines go, where one-time
--     lines go, what state the order lands in (§7.1). A deal may override it.
--     Modelling it as columns would freeze a policy shape that is still being
--     learned; modelling it as rows would invent a rules engine nobody asked
--     for. It is read and validated server-side by one remote operation.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.Pipeline (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    -- NOT NULL by design (L-14). Every deal therefore inherits a company, and
    -- Deal.CompanyID is guaranteed present for §9.2's company dimension.
    CompanyID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    -- Default deal type for deals created in this pipeline.
    DealTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    DefaultForecastCategoryTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- Simple vs. priced mode, as a pipeline-level DEFAULT (§6, L-2), overridable
    -- per deal. Partner-referral and sponsorship pipelines may never carry catalog
    -- lines; a simple deal is header-only with a hand-entered Amount and
    -- AmountIsComputed = 0. Two clean modes, not a spectrum.
    RequiresDealLines BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    -- The declarative close policy (§7.1). NVARCHAR(MAX) holding JSON.
    CloseWonPolicy NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsDefault BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    DisplayRank INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_Pipeline PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    -- PER COMPANY, not global.
    CONSTRAINT UQ_Pipeline_Company_Code UNIQUE (CompanyID, Code),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Pipeline_Company FOREIGN KEY (CompanyID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Company (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Pipeline_DealType FOREIGN KEY (DealTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Pipeline_DefaultForecastCategoryType FOREIGN KEY (DefaultForecastCategoryTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.ForecastCategoryType (ID)
);
GO

-- The other half of the DealType <-> Pipeline cycle, added now that both tables exist.
ALTER TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealType
    ADD CONSTRAINT FK_DealType_DefaultPipeline FOREIGN KEY (DefaultPipelineID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Pipeline (ID);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5.2 PipelineStage
--
--     STAGES CARRY NO IsWon / IsClosed OF THEIR OWN. They point at a
--     DealStatusType that does, via DealStatusTypeID — "the status a deal takes
--     on entering this stage". That indirection is the entire reason "Closed
--     Won" is a LABEL rather than a behaviour, and it lets a pipeline call its
--     winning stage "Signed", "Booked" or "Enrolled" with no code aware of the
--     difference.
--
--     EntryCriteria / ExitCriteria / RequiredFields are declarative JSON
--     predicates evaluated SERVER-SIDE — the same posture Sonar takes with
--     factors and Caliber with WorkflowRoute conditions. A stage that cannot be
--     left without a signed mutual action plan is a config row, not a code branch.
--
--     GuidanceMarkdown is the "what good looks like at this stage" text shown in
--     the deal workspace. It is the cheapest sales-enablement feature in
--     existence and every CRM sells it as an add-on.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.PipelineStage (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    PipelineID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    Code NVARCHAR(40) NOT NULL,
    DisplayOrder INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    -- Default probability applied to deals entering this stage. Copied onto the
    -- deal rather than read through, so a deal keeps the probability it was
    -- judged at when a stage is later retuned.
    Probability DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,

    ForecastCategoryTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- The status a deal takes on when it enters this stage. See the note above.
    DealStatusTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- Days without activity before the board flags the deal as rotting.
    RottingDays INT NULL,

    EntryCriteria NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    ExitCriteria NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    RequiredFields NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    GuidanceMarkdown NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    CONSTRAINT PK_PipelineStage PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_PipelineStage_Pipeline_Code UNIQUE (PipelineID, Code),
    CONSTRAINT FK_PipelineStage_Pipeline FOREIGN KEY (PipelineID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Pipeline (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_PipelineStage_ForecastCategoryType FOREIGN KEY (ForecastCategoryTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.ForecastCategoryType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_PipelineStage_DealStatusType FOREIGN KEY (DealStatusTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStatusType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT CK_PipelineStage_Probability
        CHECK (Probability IS NULL OR (Probability >= 0 AND Probability <= 100)),
    CONSTRAINT CK_PipelineStage_RottingDays
        CHECK (RottingDays IS NULL OR RottingDays >= 0)
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 6. THE DEAL (master plan §4.3)
-- =============================================================================

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6.1 Deal
--
--     THE THREE Amount* PROVENANCE COLUMNS ARE LOAD-BEARING — do not treat them
--     as metadata. `Amount` is a CACHED ANSWER, not a fact: it holds whatever
--     Orders.PreviewOrder last returned for this deal's line set.
--     `AmountSourceHash` fingerprints that line set, so the UI can compare it
--     against the current lines and say "this figure is stale, reprice" instead
--     of showing a number nobody can trace. `AmountIsComputed = 0` marks a
--     SIMPLE deal (L-2) whose amount was typed by a human and is not owed an
--     explanation. Remove these and Amount becomes a hand-edited field within a
--     month, and the guarantee in §6 dies quietly.
--
--     OwnerEmployeeID is a SERVER-MAINTAINED DENORMALIZED STAMP, written by
--     DealEntityServer.Save() whenever team membership changes (§5.1). The single
--     source of truth for who is on a deal — including the owner — is
--     DealTeamMember. This column exists purely so "my deals" and per-rep boards
--     do not need a join on every query. It mirrors OrderLine.CompanyID in
--     orders. NEVER hand-set it; the extended property below says so, because
--     otherwise somebody eventually will and the two will diverge silently.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.Deal (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    -- DEAL-{seq}. The sequence generator lands with the entity server; the column
    -- is nullable here so CodeGen's CRUD procs can insert before it is assigned.
    DealNumber NVARCHAR(50) NULL,
    Name NVARCHAR(500) NOT NULL,

    PipelineID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    PipelineStageID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    DealTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    DealStatusTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    AccountID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    PrimaryContactID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- The SELLING company (D-4). Must match Pipeline.CompanyID; the entity server
    -- enforces that, because a CHECK cannot reach across the FK to compare them.
    CompanyID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    -- Denormalized. Server-maintained. See the header note.
    OwnerEmployeeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- The cached, provenance-carrying amount. See the header note.
    Amount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    AmountIsComputed BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    AmountComputedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NULL,
    AmountSourceHash NVARCHAR(128) NULL,

    -- SOFT reference. Orders carries no currency columns at all (its D24 defers
    -- FX), so there is no settled target to point a foreign key at yet.
    CurrencyID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    MRR DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    ARR DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    TermMonths INT NULL,

    ExpectedCloseDate DATE NULL,
    ActualCloseDate DATE NULL,
    Probability DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,
    ForecastCategoryTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    LossReasonID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    LossNotes NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    LeadSourceTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- SOFT reference: not present in MJ core at the version this was authored
    -- against. Verify before promoting to a foreign key.
    CampaignID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    -- SOFT references DOWN to bizapps-contracts (L-15). There is deliberately no
    -- Contract.DealID going the other way: contracts sits BELOW sales so an upward
    -- reference would invert the app graph, and the cardinality is ONE contract to
    -- MANY deals — the original sale is a deal, every renewal is another, and
    -- expansions are more. A single Contract.DealID could only ever name one of
    -- them and would silently become "whichever deal we wrote last".
    ContractID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- What makes the renewal chain navigable from the sales side without contracts
    -- knowing anything about it.
    RenewsContractID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    NextStep NVARCHAR(1000) NULL,
    NextStepDate DATE NULL,

    ClosedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NULL,
    ClosedByUserID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_Deal PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_Deal_DealNumber UNIQUE (DealNumber),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_Pipeline FOREIGN KEY (PipelineID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Pipeline (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_PipelineStage FOREIGN KEY (PipelineStageID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.PipelineStage (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_DealType FOREIGN KEY (DealTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_DealStatusType FOREIGN KEY (DealStatusTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStatusType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_SalesAccount FOREIGN KEY (AccountID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.SalesAccount (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_PrimaryContact FOREIGN KEY (PrimaryContactID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.SalesContact (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_Company FOREIGN KEY (CompanyID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Company (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_OwnerEmployee FOREIGN KEY (OwnerEmployeeID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Employee (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_ForecastCategoryType FOREIGN KEY (ForecastCategoryTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.ForecastCategoryType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_LossReason FOREIGN KEY (LossReasonID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LossReason (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_LeadSourceType FOREIGN KEY (LeadSourceTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.LeadSourceType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_Deal_ClosedByUser FOREIGN KEY (ClosedByUserID)
        REFERENCES __mj.[User] (ID),

    -- STRUCTURAL invariants only.
    CONSTRAINT CK_Deal_Probability
        CHECK (Probability IS NULL OR (Probability >= 0 AND Probability <= 100)),
    CONSTRAINT CK_Deal_TermMonths
        CHECK (TermMonths IS NULL OR TermMonths >= 0)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6.2 DealLine
--
--     THE SHAPE OF THIS TABLE IS THE PRICING RULE MADE STRUCTURAL. The first
--     block is INTENT — what the rep is asking for. The second block is the
--     RESOLVED ANSWER, and it is WRITE-ONLY from this app's perspective:
--     populated only from an Orders.PreviewOrder response, never computed
--     locally, never hand-edited.
--
--     What sales must never do, restated because this is the table where it
--     would happen: multiply quantity by price · apply a discount percentage ·
--     compute tax · prorate a partial period · sum lines into a header total ·
--     round anything.
--
--     PriceComponentsJSON stores the explanation trail orders returns, so a rep
--     can answer "why is it this price" without a support ticket.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealLine (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    DealID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,

    -- ===== INTENT — what the rep is asking for =====
    -- SOFT reference to the bizapps-orders catalog (DG-6): orders' migrations may
    -- not have run, which is precisely what lets this baseline stand alone.
    ProductID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    Quantity DECIMAL(19, 4) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    RequestedDiscountPct DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,
    -- An INPUT to the pricing engine, never a replacement for it. A negotiated
    -- price still goes through PreviewOrder.
    OverrideUnitPrice DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    TermMonths INT NULL,
    ServicePeriodStart DATE NULL,
    ServicePeriodEnd DATE NULL,
    LineType NVARCHAR(40) NULL,
    DisplayOrder INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    Description NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    -- ===== RESOLVED — write-only, from Orders.PreviewOrder =====
    ResolvedUnitPrice DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    ResolvedExtendedAmount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    PriceComponentsJSON NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,
    PricedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NULL,

    -- Denormalized stamp of the PRODUCT's company at price time (§10), mirroring
    -- OrderLine.CompanyID in orders. This is what lets a cross-company deal
    -- materialize into orders with correct per-line company ownership, so each
    -- line books its own single-company journal entry with no extra work here.
    -- Server-maintained; never hand-set.
    CompanyID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealLine PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealLine_Deal FOREIGN KEY (DealID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Deal (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealLine_Company FOREIGN KEY (CompanyID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Company (ID),

    -- STRUCTURAL invariants: non-negative quantity, bounded percentage, date ordering.
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealLine_Quantity CHECK (Quantity >= 0),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealLine_RequestedDiscountPct
        CHECK (RequestedDiscountPct IS NULL OR (RequestedDiscountPct >= 0 AND RequestedDiscountPct <= 100)),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealLine_TermMonths CHECK (TermMonths IS NULL OR TermMonths >= 0),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealLine_ServicePeriodOrder
        CHECK (ServicePeriodStart IS NULL OR ServicePeriodEnd IS NULL OR ServicePeriodEnd >= ServicePeriodStart)
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6.3 DealStageEvent — the immutable transition log
--
--     APPEND-ONLY, NEVER EDITED. This single table is where stage-conversion
--     rates, velocity, dwell time, slippage and "deals that skipped
--     qualification" all come from.
--
--     STAMPING AmountAtTransition AND ProbabilityAtTransition IS THE WHOLE
--     POINT. "What is the forecast" and "what did we think the forecast was on
--     the 1st" are different questions, and the second is unanswerable from Deal
--     alone once amounts and dates change. Reconstructing history after the fact
--     is impossible without these columns, which is why they are stamped at
--     write time rather than derived later.
--
--     ChangedAt is a real column rather than a reliance on __mj_CreatedAt because
--     the HubSpot import (§12) must preserve ORIGINAL timestamps — a historical
--     transition from 2023 has to land as 2023, not as import time.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStageEvent (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    DealID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,

    -- Nullable: the FIRST event of a deal's life has no from-stage.
    FromStageID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    ToStageID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    FromDealStatusTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    ToDealStatusTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    ChangedByUserID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- The ORIGINAL time of the transition. See the header note on the importer.
    ChangedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),
    DaysInPreviousStage INT NULL,

    -- The point-in-time stamps. See the header note.
    AmountAtTransition DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    ProbabilityAtTransition DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,

    Notes NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealStageEvent PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_Deal FOREIGN KEY (DealID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Deal (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_FromStage FOREIGN KEY (FromStageID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.PipelineStage (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_ToStage FOREIGN KEY (ToStageID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.PipelineStage (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_FromDealStatusType FOREIGN KEY (FromDealStatusTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStatusType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_ToDealStatusType FOREIGN KEY (ToDealStatusTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealStatusType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealStageEvent_ChangedByUser FOREIGN KEY (ChangedByUserID)
        REFERENCES __mj.[User] (ID),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealStageEvent_DaysInPreviousStage
        CHECK (DaysInPreviousStage IS NULL OR DaysInPreviousStage >= 0),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealStageEvent_ProbabilityAtTransition
        CHECK (ProbabilityAtTransition IS NULL OR (ProbabilityAtTransition >= 0 AND ProbabilityAtTransition <= 100))
);
GO

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6.4 DealContactRole — the buying committee on the CUSTOMER side (§4.3)
--
--     A junction rather than a field on SalesContact because one contact holds
--     DIFFERENT roles on different deals: the champion of one deal is the
--     end user of the next.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealContactRole (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    DealID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    SalesContactID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    BuyingRoleTypeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    Influence DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,
    Notes NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealContactRole PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealContactRole_Deal_Contact_Role UNIQUE (DealID, SalesContactID, BuyingRoleTypeID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealContactRole_Deal FOREIGN KEY (DealID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Deal (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealContactRole_SalesContact FOREIGN KEY (SalesContactID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.SalesContact (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealContactRole_BuyingRoleType FOREIGN KEY (BuyingRoleTypeID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.BuyingRoleType (ID),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealContactRole_Influence
        CHECK (Influence IS NULL OR (Influence >= 0 AND Influence <= 100))
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 7. THE DEAL TEAM (master plan §5, L-16)
-- =============================================================================

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 7.1 DealTeamMember
--
--     THE SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for who is on a deal — INCLUDING THE OWNER,
--     who is simply the member holding a role where DealRole.IsOwnerRole = 1
--     (exactly one per deal, enforced server-side from the flag).
--     Deal.OwnerEmployeeID is a denormalized stamp of that, not a parallel truth.
--
--     D-6 RESOLVED: EmployeeID XOR PersonID.
--     The plan shipped this table with EmployeeID alone and flagged the gap as
--     schema-blocking, because `Partner Manager` is a seeded DealRole and an
--     Employee row cannot express a partner rep or a contractor — so it bites on
--     the first partner-sourced deal. Confirmed 2026-08-04: add a nullable
--     PersonID beside EmployeeID with an exactly-one-of CHECK. This is the same
--     idiom common.ContactMethod and Relationship already use for
--     Person-xor-Organization. Employee stays primary; the cost is one column and
--     one structural constraint, and retrofitting it after attribution data
--     exists would cost far more.
--
--     ⚠️ THE ATTRIBUTION TRAP (§5.2), recorded here because this is the table
--     that produces it: a deal with an AE, an SE and an SDR has THREE ROWS, and
--     SUMMING Deal.Amount ACROSS THEM TRIPLE-COUNTS THE DEAL. Every by-rep or
--     by-role rollup must either filter to the owner role (the default for
--     "bookings by rep", and the only default that reconciles to total bookings)
--     or weight by AttributionPct, which the app validates sums to 100 across a
--     deal's active members when any are set. A total that silently exceeds real
--     bookings is worse than no total.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.DealTeamMember (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),
    DealID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,

    -- Exactly one of these two is set. Employee is the common case and stays first.
    EmployeeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    -- D-6: the partner rep / contractor case. A common.Person who is not an Employee.
    PersonID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    DealRoleID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    AttributionPct DECIMAL(5, 2) NULL,
    StartDate DATE NULL,
    EndDate DATE NULL,
    IsActive BIT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    Notes NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_DealTeamMember PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    -- The plan's uniqueness rule, extended to the D-6 shape: a given person may hold a
    -- given role on a given deal once, whichever column identifies them. Two separate
    -- constraints rather than one over both columns, because NULL does not collide with
    -- NULL in a SQL Server UNIQUE constraint the way the naive single form would need.
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealTeamMember_Deal_Employee_Role UNIQUE (DealID, EmployeeID, DealRoleID),
    CONSTRAINT UQ_DealTeamMember_Deal_Person_Role UNIQUE (DealID, PersonID, DealRoleID),

    CONSTRAINT FK_DealTeamMember_Deal FOREIGN KEY (DealID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Deal (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealTeamMember_Employee FOREIGN KEY (EmployeeID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Employee (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealTeamMember_Person FOREIGN KEY (PersonID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsCommon.Person (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_DealTeamMember_DealRole FOREIGN KEY (DealRoleID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.DealRole (ID),

    -- STRUCTURAL: exactly-one-of, bounded percentage, date ordering.
    -- Spelled out as two arms rather than the tempting `(EmployeeID IS NULL) <> (PersonID IS NULL)`:
    -- T-SQL has no boolean VALUE type, so an `IS NULL` predicate cannot be an operand to `<>` and
    -- the terse form dies with "Incorrect syntax near '<'". This is also the exact shape
    -- common.ContactMethod and Relationship use for Person-xor-Organization, which is the idiom
    -- D-6 said to follow — and it converts to PostgreSQL unchanged.
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealTeamMember_EmployeeXorPerson CHECK (
        (EmployeeID IS NOT NULL AND PersonID IS NULL) OR
        (EmployeeID IS NULL AND PersonID IS NOT NULL)
    ),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealTeamMember_AttributionPct
        CHECK (AttributionPct IS NULL OR (AttributionPct >= 0 AND AttributionPct <= 100)),
    CONSTRAINT CK_DealTeamMember_DateOrder
        CHECK (StartDate IS NULL OR EndDate IS NULL OR EndDate >= StartDate)
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 8. FORECAST SNAPSHOTS (master plan §9.5)
-- =============================================================================

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 8.1 ForecastSnapshot
--
--     Written by a Scheduled Job. SNAPSHOTS MATTER MORE THAN THE LIVE NUMBER:
--     "what did we think on the first of the month" is the question a forecast
--     review actually asks, and it is unanswerable after the fact without them.
--     The live figure reads Deal; the historical one reads this table (§9.4).
--
--     COLUMN-NAMING DEVIATION FROM THE PLAN, deliberate and worth stating.
--     The master plan names these columns `Commit`, `BestCase`, `Pipeline`,
--     `Closed`. They ship here as `CommitAmount`, `BestCaseAmount`,
--     `PipelineAmount`, `ClosedAmount` because COMMIT is a reserved word in both
--     T-SQL and PostgreSQL, and production is PostgreSQL. A bare `Commit` column
--     would need quoting in every generated view, procedure and converted
--     statement for the life of the app — and `Pipeline` colliding with the
--     Pipeline table's name reads as a bug to every future reader besides. The
--     `*Amount` suffix also says what the numbers ARE, which the bare names did
--     not. Flagged for confirmation.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE TABLE __mj_BizAppsSales.ForecastSnapshot (
    ID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL DEFAULT NEWSEQUENTIALID(),

    CompanyID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NOT NULL,
    PipelineID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,
    OwnerEmployeeID UNIQUEIDENTIFIER NULL,

    PeriodStart DATE NOT NULL,
    PeriodEnd DATE NOT NULL,
    CapturedAt DATETIMEOFFSET NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME(),

    -- The four roll-up buckets. Renamed from the plan — see the header note.
    CommitAmount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    BestCaseAmount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    PipelineAmount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,
    ClosedAmount DECIMAL(19, 4) NULL,

    -- The full breakdown behind the four numbers, so a snapshot can be
    -- interrogated rather than merely displayed.
    SnapshotJSON NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,

    CONSTRAINT PK_ForecastSnapshot PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_ForecastSnapshot_Company FOREIGN KEY (CompanyID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Company (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_ForecastSnapshot_Pipeline FOREIGN KEY (PipelineID)
        REFERENCES __mj_BizAppsSales.Pipeline (ID),
    CONSTRAINT FK_ForecastSnapshot_OwnerEmployee FOREIGN KEY (OwnerEmployeeID)
        REFERENCES __mj.Employee (ID),
    CONSTRAINT CK_ForecastSnapshot_PeriodOrder CHECK (PeriodEnd >= PeriodStart)
);
GO


-- =============================================================================
-- 9. EXTENDED PROPERTIES — column and table documentation
-- =============================================================================
-- CodeGen lifts MS_Description into __mj.Entity.Description and
-- __mj.EntityField.Description, so this is what a user sees as help text in Explorer and what an
-- MJ agent reads to understand the model. Written for the columns whose PURPOSE is not evident
-- from the name — especially the ones with a rule attached, because a rule that lives only in a
-- migration comment is a rule the next person breaks.

-- DealStatusType
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'The OUTCOME vocabulary for deals (Open, Won, Lost, Abandoned, On Hold ...). Behaviour comes from the flags on this row, NEVER from the Name — Sales.CloseDeal is named for the outcome type rather than a hardcoded "won" precisely so it can resolve everything from IsWon/IsLost/LocksDeal. Renaming a status is a metadata change with no code impact.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealStatusType';
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'Stable identifier the engine and metadata seeds key on. Renaming Name is cosmetic; renaming Code changes an identifier.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealStatusType', @level2type=N'COLUMN', @level2name=N'Code';
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'When 1, a deal in this status FREEZES: the header (except Description and NextStep), its lines and its team members become immutable, enforced in DealEntityServer.Save() — not in the UI, so an Action, an agent or a raw BaseEntity.Save() hits the same wall. Mirrors journal-entry immutability in accounting, and for the same reason: the deal is now the provenance of a contract and an order.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealStatusType', @level2type=N'COLUMN', @level2name=N'LocksDeal';
GO

-- DealRole
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'The roles internal people hold on a deal (Owner/AE, Sales Engineer, SDR, Executive Sponsor, Partner Manager, CS Lead).', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealRole';
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'Identifies the role that DEFINES ownership. Exactly one team member per deal may hold a role with this flag, enforced server-side, and Deal.OwnerEmployeeID is the denormalized stamp of whoever does.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealRole', @level2type=N'COLUMN', @level2name=N'IsOwnerRole';
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'Whether two people may hold this role on one deal. Two sales engineers, yes; two owners, no. Enforced server-side FROM THIS FLAG, never hardcoded against a role name.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'DealRole', @level2type=N'COLUMN', @level2name=N'AllowsMultiplePerDeal';
GO

-- ForecastCategoryType
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'How a deal rolls into the forecast (Omitted, Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed). The Include* flags are read directly by the forecast measures; a query that compared a category NAME would be exactly the violation the CI grep exists to catch.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'ForecastCategoryType';
GO

-- LossReason
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'Why a deal was lost. Loss reason is this app''s ONLY mandatory field and the friction is deliberate: loss reasons are the highest-value and most consistently-skipped data in any CRM.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'LossReason';
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'When 1, Sales.CloseDeal refuses a close against this reason unless Deal.LossNotes is supplied.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'LossReason', @level2type=N'COLUMN', @level2name=N'RequiresNotes';
GO

-- LifecycleStageType
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty @name=N'MS_Description', @value=N'Where a Person or Organization sits on the journey from stranger to evangelist. This table is what makes "Lead" a STAGE rather than an entity: a lead is a common.Person carrying a LifecycleStageTypeID, not a second identity table for humans.', @level0type=N'SCHEMA', @level0name=N'__mj_BizAppsSales', @level1type=N'TABLE', @level1name=N'LifecycleStageType';
